---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---

<DocLayout title="Navigation" description="Moving between steps, conditionally, and safely.">
  <h1>Navigation</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Four ways to move: <code>next()</code>, <code>previous()</code>, <code>goTo()</code>, and
    jumping straight to a start step.
  </p>

  <h2>The basics</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`await tour.next();                       // forward
await tour.previous();                   // back
await tour.goTo("home.settings");        // jump to a target identifier

tour.getCurrentStepIndex();              // position in the active steps
tour.getCurrentStepSourceIndex();        // position in config.steps
tour.getCurrentStep();                   // the step object, or null`}
  />

  <Callout title="Two indexes, on purpose">
    <p>
      <code>getCurrentStepIndex()</code> counts steps that actually applied to this run, after
      conditional steps were excluded. <code>getCurrentStepSourceIndex()</code> is the index in
      your authored <code>steps</code> array, so you can map an event back to configuration.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Navigation is asynchronous</h2>

  <p>
    <code>next()</code>, <code>previous()</code> and <code>goTo()</code> return promises,
    because a step may need to wait for its target to appear. Await them in application code when
    the next thing you do depends on the new step being on screen.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`async function openStep(id) {
  await tour.goTo(id);
  // The new step is rendered and positioned here.
  analytics.track("tour_step_viewed", { id });
}`}
  />

  <h2>Starting somewhere specific</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({ tourId: "x", startAt: 2, steps: [/* … */] });      // by index
createTour({ tourId: "x", startAt: "home.cta", steps: [/* … */] }); // by identifier`}
  />

  <p>
    If the identifier does not exist — or the index is past the end after conditional steps are
    applied — <code>start()</code> rejects with a <code>TourConfigError</code> naming what it
    expected.
  </p>

  <h2>Looping</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({ tourId: "x", loop: true, steps: [/* … */] });

await tour.next();  // last step -> wraps to the first
await tour.previous();  // first step -> wraps to the last`}
  />

  <h2>Conditional steps</h2>

  <p>
    <code>when</code> decides whether a step belongs to this run. It is evaluated once at start, and
    the predicate may be asynchronous.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "conditional",
  steps: [
    { tourId: "always", title: "Shown", when: true },
    { tourId: "never", title: "Hidden", when: false },
    { tourId: "predicate", title: "Conditional", when: () => hasBillingEnabled },
    { tourId: "async", title: "Awaited", when: async () => await checkFeature("beta") },
  ],
});

// A predicate that throws rejects start() with a message naming the step index,
// rather than silently including or excluding it.
`}
  />

  <h2>Optional steps</h2>

  <p>
    An <code>optional</code> step may be jumped over by <code>goTo()</code>. Sequential
    <code>next()</code> still visits it — the difference is that an explicit jump from the host
    application may skip it.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  steps: [
    { tourId: "intro", title: "Start" },
    { tourId: "advanced", title: "Advanced tips", optional: true },
    { tourId: "outro", title: "Finish" },
  ],
});

// These visit advanced:
//   await tour.next();
//
// This skips it:
//   await tour.goTo("outro");
//`}
  />

  <h2>Rapid input</h2>

  <p>
    Users double-click. Each navigation takes a sequence number, and a superseded one abandons
    itself instead of painting over its successor.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`// Five clicks, four steps. Deterministic outcome, one overlay, no flicker.
await Promise.allSettled([tour.next(), tour.next(), tour.next(), tour.next(), tour.next()]);

tour.getState();  // "completed"
document.querySelectorAll(".tat-root").length;  // 0`}
  />

  <h2>Skipping a step whose target is missing</h2>

  <p>
    With <code>target.missing: "skip"</code>, a step whose target cannot be found is stepped over
    automatically. The skip applies to the first step too, and if <em>every</em> step is skipped
    the tour reports that there is nothing to show rather than opening an empty overlay.
  </p>
</DocLayout>